---
title: A prescribing engine that the model does not control
org: Sirka
period: 2025–2026
role: I wrote the decision engine, the agent layer around it, and the shadow-mode and backtest tooling used to validate changes to it. Analytics, media and marketing features in the same codebase were built by colleagues.
---

A patient-facing assessment for a weight-management programme: it collects clinical information
through a conversation, then does one of three things — recommend a regimen, route the case to a
doctor for review, or stop. The third option matters as much as the first.

This is private company code. The clinical logic, eligibility criteria, drug names and
thresholds are not described here; what follows is the architecture and how changes to it were
validated.

## The design rule

> The LLM orchestrates. It never makes a clinical decision.

Everything else follows from that sentence. A deterministic engine holds the eligibility matrix,
the interaction rules and a universal safety gate that hard-stops on a defined set of
conditions. The language model's job is to conduct the conversation and call tools.

Concretely, the model gets a handful of read-only tools — look up what is available, ask what a
given option requires, check a proposed combination, request a specific input from the patient.
It cannot write a decision. It also cannot declare itself finished: the backend computes the
final verdict regardless of what the model says, because a model that can announce completion
can announce the wrong completion. Tool-call budgets and timeouts bound each turn, and every
error path — a timeout, a malformed response, an unmapped condition — falls through to doctor
review. No failure mode ends in an automatic prescription.

## Narrowing the model's role further

The second version took more away from the model, which I think was the right direction.

Code owns the order of questions. The model no longer decides what to ask next; it extracts
fields from free text under a forced JSON schema and phrases the replies. Safety-critical fields
cannot be filled from conversation at all — they are settable only through interface widgets,
under a default-deny allowlist, so a confident misreading of a sentence cannot set the field
that determines eligibility. A plausibility gate checks the physical measurements before they
reach the engine, because a transposed height is not a clinical question but it produces a
clinical answer.

The pattern that generalises: use the model for what it is good at, which is understanding messy
human text and producing fluent replies, and keep the state machine in code where you can test
it exhaustively.

## Validating a change to clinical logic

This is the part I would most want to talk through, because changing an engine that affects
prescribing is where the engineering gets genuinely difficult. A test suite tells you the new
version does what you specified. It does not tell you how the new version differs from the one
currently running on real patients.

So engine changes shipped behind a **shadow mode**. The new engine ran passively against live
sessions, decided nothing, and logged where it diverged from the engine in production. Divergence
alone is expected — that is usually the point of the change. The invariant worth enforcing is
narrower: flag any case where the new engine is *less restrictive* than the old one. A
disagreement is a review item; a safety regression is a release blocker. Separating those two
classes of difference was what made the signal usable, because otherwise a large intentional
change buries the one divergence that matters.

Alongside that, a backtest command replays historical sessions through a candidate engine, so a
change could be evaluated against accumulated real cases before it saw any live traffic. On
recent sessions the reported result was a complete match on recommended sets with zero
safety-looser divergences.

Supporting this:

- A **release-blocking gate** in all three deployment workflows, not an advisory check.
- **Shared fixtures** across the Go engine and the two TypeScript implementations, so the same
  cases must produce the same verdicts in every language the logic exists in — the alternative
  is three engines that agree until they don't.
- High statement coverage on the engine, which is cheap to achieve on pure decision logic and
  worth insisting on there specifically.
- **Decision records** for each significant revision, and a tracked log of gaps between the
  written clinical specification and the implemented engine. Some revisions sat pending ethics
  committee sign-off. Keeping the gaps in a file where a clinician could read them was more
  useful than quietly closing them.

## Related work in the same service

A combination-safety and substitution API: given a proposed regimen, check it for interactions,
and where a cheaper equivalent exists, select one subject to contraindications and
compatibility. The conversation agent in the clinic's CRM consumes this rather than reasoning
about substitutions itself — same principle, enforced across a service boundary.

I also built a close-rate analysis tool, which segments won and lost leads by routing outcome,
engine version and the flags raised during assessment, to test two hypotheses: that escalating a
case to doctor review suppresses conversion, and that leads drop off when they are moved between
platforms. It runs on a daily schedule. **I am not quoting results, because none were recorded
in a form I can verify.** The design I will stand behind; the findings I would have to re-run.

## Stack

Go, PostgreSQL, hosted models through OpenRouter, OpenTelemetry, Docker, GitHub Actions.
